 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources. The dominant cost is backpropagation through the rendering pipeline for each scalar value (or batch thereof) [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(256 \cdot r^2)$ to store the sensitivity tensor $\Sigma$ [S1].
-*   **Performance:** GPU acceleration with minibatches of size 64 is used to mitigate latency [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Provides immediate visual feedback on where TF edits will have the most impact, aiding in precise tuning of opacity TFs [S1]. Complements Region Sensitivity Plots by offering the inverse perspective (TF-to-Image vs. Image-to-TF) [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires a differentiable rendering pipeline or generative model. High-resolution sensitivity is computationally prohibitive [S1]. The method is specific to opacity TFs and may not directly apply to color or emission TFs without extension [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Proportional to $256 \times r^2$ sensitivity computations, where each computation requires a backpropagation pass through the volume renderer [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(256 \times r^2)$ to store the tensor $\Sigma$ [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Setting $r=256$ is prohibitively costly due to the $256^2$ backpropagation passes required [S1]. Using $r=8$ or $16$ significantly reduces latency [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Allows users to understand the impact of TF edits on specific image regions before applying them; complements Region Sensitivity Plots [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Computationally expensive for high-resolution grids; sensitivity values are relative and may require careful opacity encoding to be visible [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Scalar Value Sensitivity Fields were introduced in the context of generative models for volume rendering, specifically in the paper "A Generative Model for Volume Rendering" [S1]. The method leverages the latent space encoding of opacity TFs learned by a generative adversarial network (GAN) to compute sensitivities [S1]. It was developed to help users explore the opacity TF latent space and understand the relationship between TF modifications and rendered image changes [S1]. The technique is part of a broader interface that combines Region Sensitivity Plots and Scalar Value Sensitivity Fields for interactive TF design [S1].
+Scalar Value Sensitivity Fields were introduced in the paper "A Generative Model for Volume Rendering" [S1]. The method is part of a broader framework for exploring opacity transfer function latent spaces using generative models (specifically GANs) [S1]. It is used in conjunction with Region Sensitivity Plots to provide a comprehensive interface for TF editing and exploration [S1].
 
